# Sandbox Hub

Several purpose-built sandboxes, each its own tool with its own instruction and
container. Servers stay in `SANDBOX_PROFILES_FILE`. Users only turn profiles on
or off (`/sandbox`); agents opt in from the tool picker. Admins listed in
`SANDBOX_HUB_ADMINS` add, edit, order, and delete profiles, prepare images, and
edit each profile's instruction for every user. Maintainer doc:
`openwiki/architecture/sandbox.md` (Sandbox Hub profiles, Editable instructions).

## Invariants (keep)

- **`general` is the old Sandbox.** It keeps `lobe-sandbox`, the legacy
  alias, the built-in instruction (as its starting text), and the pre-profile
  session key. Without a profiles file or a saved edit its manifest is
  byte-identical to the old static
  `SandboxManifest` (golden test in `payload.sandbox.test.ts`).
- **Profile ids:** `^[a-z][a-z0-9]*(?:-[a-z0-9]+)*$`, ≤ 16 chars, never `_`.
  Longer ids push `lobe-sandbox--<id>____<api>____builtin` past 64 chars, where
  `ToolNameResolver` hashes names and the worker (`split('____')`) breaks.
- **Sandbox tools are appended last** to the builtin list (worker payload and
  tool-store slice), where the static manifest used to be. Do not reorder:
  it changes prompt bytes for every existing agent.
- **One gate:** every call goes through `resolveSandboxProfileForUser`. Do not
  add a path that invokes the provider without it.
- **Server details never reach the browser:** the user profile list has no URL,
  key, or image. The admin editor includes the image name. Keys come from
  `apiKeyEnv`; a literal key in the file is rejected.
- **Profiles come from the database.** Read them with the async
  `getSandboxProfileCatalog(db)` (`profiles/hub.ts`); `getSandboxFileCatalog()`
  is only servers, the import source, and the missing-table fallback. Import
  file profiles only while the Hub table is empty. Never cache profiles per
  process: an admin edit must apply on the next call everywhere.
- **Profile writes are admin only and file-rule checked** (`hubAdmin.ts`,
  `sandboxProfileConfigSchema`). Ids never change after creation. `general`
  and the last profile cannot be deleted.
- **Secrets stay in env.** Server keys (`apiKeyEnv`) and registry logins
  (`registries[].usernameEnv/passwordEnv`) are never written to the
  database, the Hub DTOs, or logs.
- **A broken profiles file configures nothing.** Do not "recover" by running
  the valid half; the operator would not notice the missing server.
- **Instructions: starting text plus a shared edit.** `compose.ts` builds the
  starting text from the stored fields. The same fields always produce the
  same bytes. A Hub read composes them again, so an admin edit applies on the
  next call. A row in `sandbox_profile_instructions` replaces that text for
  **every** user until reset. No per-user or per-request text in an
  instruction. Without a saved row the prompt bytes must stay exactly the
  starting text (golden test).
- **Instruction writes are admin only.** `SandboxProfileInstructionModel` is
  not user-scoped; every write path must call `isSandboxHubAdmin` first.
- **Fail closed on reads.** Only a missing table (`42P01`) may fall back to
  defaults (choices) or the starting text (instructions). Any other read
  error is raised, never treated as "no rows".
- **Background jobs** (`jobs/`, `sandbox_jobs`): every job call and every
  watcher run goes through `resolveSandboxProfileForUser`; the watcher never
  creates a sandbox (`createIfMissing: false`); every job has a deadline;
  only `SandboxJobModel.finish` ends a job and only while it is running.
  Write the job row only after `withWorkspace` returns. Catch and note any
  database or storage error inside the task: a non-request error that leaves
  the task makes the provider delete the sandbox and kills the job.
- Hub tRPC lives in the `sandboxHub.` namespace, which is in the client's
  `VERIFIED_ACCOUNT_RPC_NAMESPACES`. A new scope-checked namespace must be
  added there too, or every call fails as a mismatched user.

## Remote servers

ChatHub reaches execd only through the lifecycle server proxy, so a dedicated
host exposes one HTTPS endpoint. OpenSandbox has no TLS and its single-tenant
proxy route is not key-checked: the reverse proxy must require
`OPEN-SANDBOX-API-KEY` on every path and allow only the ChatHub host
(`docker/opensandbox-remote/`). Server-wide settings (runtime class, egress
mode, `network_mode`, max timeout) need separate servers.

## Verify

Provider, session-key, or label changes: run `e2e.live.test.ts` 5+ times on a
local OpenSandbox (`.cursor/rules/sandbox-repro.mdc`), including the
two-profiles-in-one-conversation case, and confirm no containers are left.
